					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/nederbird" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2854162); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2854162&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607460152" alt="Nederbird" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2854162)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/nederbird" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2854162); return false;" >Nederbird</a></span> <span class="odate time_1501527321 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">31 Jul 2017 18:55</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3562009">
									

<p>This is great stuff! The worldbuilding you've done is amazing, and I'm glad to see the Daevites get more articles. I really like the Ortothan material too.</p>
<p>The only thing that bugged me with this was the the Century Conquest happened earlier than 10.000 BCE, especially since they apparently fought &quot;several Asian civilizations&quot;. Considering that most civilizations didn't arise until <strong>after</strong> 10.000 BCE, as that's roughly the time the Agricultural Revolution happened, it just seems strange that there wouldn't be any geological indications of agriculture or large scale horticulture if the Daevites had such a large empire. Besides, what civilizations would they have been fighting against? Besides the Ortothans, that is.</p>
<p>I'd suggest putting the years a bit later. You could probably put it between 8.000 and 3.000 BCE and still have it elude recorded history, considering that the oldest deciphered writing system dates from around -3.000. A plus is you could also use the timeframe to add a bit of immersion, maybe by saying that the Daevite-Ortothan conflict caused the collapse of the Indus Valley Civilization (the cause of which is still contested today).</p>

							</div>

								
					
